ines separated by only 0.6 nm. Meanwhile, the Stern-Gerlach experiment yielded a puzzling result: a beam of silver atoms split cleanly into exactly two beams in an inhomogeneous magnetic field, rather than the odd number of beams ","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"bud6LRaqL9"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"2l+1","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2l+1</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7778em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.01968em;\">l</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span>","key":"As3s5pIX6I"},{"type":"text","value":" expected from orbital angular momentum quantization. These phenomena could not be explained by the three quantum numbers ","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"sitgfAdgVn"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"(n, l, m)","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(n, l, m)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html